### stage-refs-curator/SKILL.md
--- 1
**Fanning the read out (Principle 9).** More than one paper in a run — an `add` list, or the batch a `discover` run's user picked — splits one delegate per paper — writing sub-agents, dispatched in a single message so they run concurrently. No question precedes it (§6.1); a host that offers no dispatch, or refuses the call, falls back to reading them here, and the digest says so. Items 1 and 2 still run at home — the bibliographic record is what the entry is transcribed from, and no delegate fetches one — but not for the whole list before the first reader starts. Per paper: resolve the record, transcribe the entry, assign the note's filename, dispatch that paper's reader, then begin the next paper's record. The two streams reach different hosts — records to DBLP and Crossref, pages to arXiv and the venue sites — so neither spends the other's rate and a backoff on one no longer stops the other, where resolving every record first left every reader idle through a rate-limit storm at DBLP. Item 3 is what travels, and each brief carries its paper's page candidates in the order item 3 names them, the seconds it waits between its own requests to a host (`references/source-policy.md`, Rate limits), its own cache prefix under `wkdrs/refs_<date>/raw/`, the note path it is to write, the collector contract verbatim, the story and the claim ledger so `## Relation to ours` is written where the paper was read, and the scope line "ONLY this paper; fetch only its pages; write only that one note file". At involve `high`, announce the partition before dispatch (§6.8). Item 4 then runs at home over what came back, per paper: the index row and the `% src:` line, the impact score, and the batch self-check — all in files no delegate opens. A return whose `floor_evidence` says `not reached` wrote no note and leaves the bib entry standing alone, which is item 3's unfetchable path in fanned-out form; a return claiming the floor was reached while naming no note file contradicts itself, and that paper is read again here (§6.3).
+++ 1
**Fanning the read out (Principle 9).** More than one paper in a run — an `add` list, or the batch a `discover` run's user picked — splits one delegate per paper — `Agent` subagents (`subagent_type: general-purpose`), dispatched in a single message so they run concurrently. No question precedes it (§6.1); a host that offers no dispatch, or refuses the call, falls back to reading them here, and the digest says so. Items 1 and 2 still run at home — the bibliographic record is what the entry is transcribed from, and no delegate fetches one — but not for the whole list before the first reader starts. Per paper: resolve the record, transcribe the entry, assign the note's filename, dispatch that paper's reader, then begin the next paper's record. The two streams reach different hosts — records to DBLP and Crossref, pages to arXiv and the venue sites — so neither spends the other's rate and a backoff on one no longer stops the other, where resolving every record first left every reader idle through a rate-limit storm at DBLP. Item 3 is what travels, and each brief carries its paper's page candidates in the order item 3 names them, the seconds it waits between its own requests to a host (`references/source-policy.md`, Rate limits), its own cache prefix under `wkdrs/refs_<date>/raw/`, the note path it is to write, the collector contract verbatim, the story and the claim ledger so `## Relation to ours` is written where the paper was read, and the scope line "ONLY this paper; fetch only its pages; write only that one note file". At involve `high`, announce the partition before dispatch (§6.8). Item 4 then runs at home over what came back, per paper: the index row and the `% src:` line, the impact score, and the batch self-check — all in files no delegate opens. A return whose `floor_evidence` says `not reached` wrote no note and leaves the bib entry standing alone, which is item 3's unfetchable path in fanned-out form; a return claiming the floor was reached while naming no note file contradicts itself, and that paper is read again here (§6.3).
### stage-refs-curator/SKILL_zh.md
--- 1
**把阅读扇出去（原则 9）。** 一次运行里多于一篇——一份 `add` 清单，或 `discover` 那一轮用户挑中的一批——就按一篇论文一个委派者拆开——用your sub-agent tool 子代理（writing），在同一条消息里一次派出去，它们才是并行的。前面不加提问（§6.1）；宿主根本不提供派发、或者拒绝了这次调用，就退回来在这里读，并在摘要里说明。第 1、2 步仍在家里跑——条目是照着书目记录誊的，而委派者不抓记录——但不是整份清单全跑完才轮到第一个读者开工。逐篇来：解析记录、誊写条目、定下笔记的文件名、把这一篇的读者派出去，然后才开始下一篇的记录。这两条流打的不是同一批主机——记录走 DBLP 与 Crossref，页面走 arXiv 与各 venue 站点——所以谁也不花谁的速率，一边退避不再让另一边停摆；而"先把每条记录都解析完"的做法，会让 DBLP 一场限流风暴期间所有读者全程空转。出门的是第 3 步，每份任务书带着它那篇论文的候选页面（顺序照第 3 步点的名）、它对某台 host 两次自己的请求之间等多少秒（`references/source-policy_zh.md`，限速一节）、它自己在 `wkdrs/refs_<date>/raw/` 下的缓存前缀、它要写的那个笔记路径、逐字的收集者合同、故事与主张记录表（这样 `## Relation to ours` 就在读到论文的那个地方写），以及范围那一句"只读这一篇；只抓它自己的页面；只写那一个笔记文件"。involve 为 `high` 时，派发之前先把分工说出来（§6.8）。第 4 步随后拿回来的东西逐篇在家里跑：索引行、`% src:` 那一行、影响力分与整批自查——全在没有委派者会打开的文件里。`floor_evidence` 写着 `not reached` 的那一份没有写出笔记，让 bib 条目单独留着——那就是第 3 步里正文抓不到那条路的扇出形态；而一份声称够到了阅读底线、却报不出笔记文件的返回是自相矛盾的，那篇论文在这里重读一遍（§6.3）。
+++ 1
